{
  "result": "I have always liked the hour just before the city fully wakes. The streets are quiet, the air feels newly washed, and even familiar buildings look briefly uncertain, as if they have not yet decided who they will be today. I walk without a destination, carrying coffee that is too hot to drink, listening to my shoes make a small, steady music against the pavement. In that looseness, thoughts arrive in a different order than they do at my desk. A half remembered song becomes a question about my childhood kitchen. A shop sign becomes a story about a stranger who once missed a train and, by missing it, met the person who changed everything. Maybe that is what freedom feels like at ordinary scale: not escape, not grandeur, but permission for the mind to wander without immediately being asked to produce something useful. The world is generous in the morning because it has not yet been narrowed by explanations. Pigeons argue over crumbs as if history depends on it. An old man sweeps his doorway with more tenderness than most people reserve for important ceremonies. A child presses both palms to a bakery window, fogging the glass, completely unaware that longing can be this simple and this complete. I want to remember that attention itself is a form of kindness. It asks nothing, fixes nothing, and yet it changes the temperature of a moment. By the time traffic thickens, I am ready to return, slightly slower inside, at last.",
